E = [
("05-01","'Sunshine of Your Love' came from a bass riff heard at a gig","Rock","1967","Jack Bruce wrote the riff after seeing Jimi Hendrix play live and going home to his bass at dawn.\n\nCream had gone to see Hendrix at a London club, and Bruce came away rattled. By his account he went home in the early hours, picked up a double bass, and worked out the descending figure that opens the song more or less immediately — a direct response to having watched someone reset the standard for what a guitarist could do.\n\nThe riff is unusual in that it is a bass line the guitar doubles rather than a guitar riff with bass underneath, which is why it sits so low and heavy.\n\nGinger Baker's drum part is the other distinctive element. Rather than marking the backbeat, he plays a tom pattern across the beat that he described as drawing on Native American rhythms, and the effect is that the song does not push forward so much as circle.\n\nThe band nearly discarded it. Eric Clapton in particular reportedly thought it too simple, and it took Ahmet Ertegun at Atlantic to insist it was a single.\n\nIt became Cream's biggest American hit and one of the most recognisable riffs in rock, which is a reasonable return on a sleepless night.",["Ginger Baker's tom pattern was inspired by Native American rhythms","The band nearly rejected it as too simple"],"guitar","story",{}),
("05-02","Which 'Blinded by the Light' wins: Springsteen or Manfred Mann?","Rock","1973","Springsteen's original is a wordy folk-rock rush. Manfred Mann's Earth Band slowed it, changed a lyric and scored the only Springsteen-written US number one.\n\nThe original, from Greetings from Asbury Park, is early Springsteen at his most verbally dense — a torrent of internal rhyme delivered at speed, closer to Dylan than to anything he would do later. It sold almost nothing.\n\nManfred Mann's Earth Band rebuilt it three years later as a prog-inflected arrangement with a long instrumental introduction, a slower tempo, and considerably more space around the words. It went to number one in America.\n\nThe famous misheard lyric belongs to that version. Springsteen wrote 'cut loose like a deuce'; Mann sang 'revved up like a deuce', and the combination of the phrasing and the arrangement has produced decades of listeners hearing something anatomical instead.\n\nSpringsteen has joked about the situation more than once: the only American number one he has written as a songwriter is a record by somebody else, and it contains a lyric he did not write.\n\nThe pairing is a decent argument that tempo is a compositional decision. The words are largely the same and the second version is legible in a way the first is not, simply because the listener is given room to follow it.",["Springsteen has joked he never had a chart-topper with his own version","The famous misheard lyric comes from Mann's rewrite"],"guitar","versus",{"a":"Bruce Springsteen (1973)","b":"Manfred Mann (1976)"}),
("05-03","James Jamerson never wrote his basslines down","Soul","1936","Motown's uncredited bassist improvised most parts on first take. Because nothing was notated, many lines were lost when he died.\n\nHe came out of Detroit jazz and approached the bass as a melodic instrument rather than a rhythmic one. His lines move constantly, use chromatic passing tones and syncopation that no arranger would have written, and frequently work against the vocal rather than supporting it — which is precisely why Motown records feel busier and more alive than their contemporaries.\n\nHe played with a single index finger, nicknamed the Hook, on a Fender Precision he refused to have cleaned, with strings he never changed.\n\nAlmost none of it was notated. He improvised parts on the first or second take and moved to the next session, so the record is the only document. Bass players have spent fifty years transcribing his playing by ear.\n\nHe appears on more number one singles than the Beatles, the Beach Boys, and Elvis Presley combined, and he was not credited on album sleeves for most of his career. The Funk Brothers as a group were essentially anonymous by label policy.\n\nWhen Motown moved to Los Angeles in 1972, most of the band was left behind. Jamerson's later years were difficult and he died in 1983, at forty-seven.",["He appears on more number ones than the Beatles and Elvis combined","He was not credited on album sleeves for most of his career"],"guitar","producer",{}),
("05-04","'Sing Sing' delivered the drums for a hip-hop decade","Hip-Hop","1976","Gaz's 'Sing Sing' and its cousins supplied breaks that New York DJs guarded like secrets, soaking labels off records to hide titles.\n\nThe secrecy was competitive and total. A DJ's value at a block party rested on having breaks nobody else could identify, so records were carried in blank sleeves and their labels were soaked off in water or scratched into illegibility. Rivals would push toward the turntables during a set specifically to read a label, and getting close enough was a genuine victory.\n\nThat culture is the direct origin of crate digging as a discipline. Finding a usable break meant listening through entire albums of unpromising material for a few seconds of drums, in thrift stores, church sales, and the bargain bins of shops that had no idea what they were holding.\n\nBreak compilations began appearing in the eighties — bootleg records collecting the useful passages in one place — which democratised access and infuriated the DJs who had done the searching.\n\nWhat the practice produced was an enormous act of collective musical archaeology. Records that had failed commercially and been forgotten were rediscovered, recontextualised, and given a second life entirely detached from their original purpose.\n\nMost of the musicians who played on them never knew.",["DJs soaked or scratched off labels to protect their sources","Digging culture grew directly from that secrecy"],"turntable","sample",{"src":"Gaz - Sing Sing (1976)","hits":["Numerous early NYC block-party routines","Later golden-era loops","Break compilations of the eighties"]}),
("05-05","Bo Diddley built his own rectangular guitars","Blues","1955","Diddley designed square-bodied guitars because standard shapes were awkward when he jumped around on stage. His clave-derived rhythm became a genre unto itself.\n\nThe square shape was ergonomic rather than decorative. He moved constantly on stage, and a conventional guitar body swung awkwardly and dug into him; a rectangle balanced better and stayed put. He built early versions himself in a workshop before Gretsch began making them for him.\n\nThe rhythm is the larger legacy. The Bo Diddley beat — three strokes, rest, two strokes — is closely related to the Afro-Cuban clave, and it arrives in his records via the long transmission of African rhythm through the Caribbean into American music. It is a pattern rather than a chord sequence, which means a song can be built on it with essentially no harmonic movement at all.\n\nHundreds of records use it: Buddy Holly's 'Not Fade Away', the Rolling Stones, the Who, U2, George Michael, and an enormous quantity of garage rock.\n\nHe was also an early user of tremolo and distortion, and his guitar sound was considerably more aggressive than most of what surrounded it in 1955.\n\nLike most Black artists of his generation on Chess, he was paid badly and spent decades saying so publicly and without much diplomacy.",["The 'Bo Diddley beat' underpins hundreds of rock songs","He built early guitars himself in a workshop"],"guitar","story",{}),
("05-06","Sam Cooke wrote 'A Change Is Gonna Come' after hearing Dylan","Soul","1964","Cooke was struck that a white songwriter had written 'Blowin' in the Wind' before he had written anything like it, and responded within months.\n\nHis reaction, as reported by people around him, was closer to embarrassment than admiration. He thought the song was good and that it should not have taken a young white folk singer to write it — that this was material he had a stronger claim to and had been avoiding.\n\nThe avoidance was commercial and rational. Cooke had built a crossover career singing to white audiences, and explicitly political material threatened it. He had also, shortly before writing the song, been arrested with his band for trying to register at a whites-only motel in Louisiana.\n\nThe arrangement is unusual for R&B of the period: a full string and horn score with timpani, closer to film music than to a soul single, which gives the song a weight the radio format did not normally permit.\n\nHe was reportedly uneasy performing it live, describing it as too personal, and it was played publicly only a handful of times before his death.\n\nCooke was shot and killed in December 1964 in circumstances that remain disputed. The song was released as a single days after his funeral.",["He was nervous performing it live, calling it too personal","The string arrangement was unusually cinematic for R&B"],"strings","writer",{}),
("05-07","'La Di Da Di' is a rap record with no instruments","Hip-Hop","1985","Doug E. Fresh beatboxed the entire backing track while Slick Rick rapped over it. Nothing else appears on the recording.\n\nThere is no drum machine, no sample, and no instrument on the record. Doug E. Fresh produces the entire rhythm track with his mouth — kick, snare, hi-hat, and a bassline — while Slick Rick delivers a narrative verse over the top, and both performances are essentially live.\n\nBeatboxing was a practical solution before it was an art form. Crews without equipment needed a beat, and a human being was the cheapest available drum machine. Doug E. Fresh was the performer who demonstrated it could be the record rather than a substitute for one.\n\nSlick Rick's contribution is the other half. His delivery is conversational, comic, and built around storytelling with character voices, and it established a narrative style that a large part of the genre followed.\n\nThe record has been sampled, interpolated, and rebuilt hundreds of times. Snoop Dogg's 'Lodi Dodi' reproduces the lyrics almost wholesale, Biggie's 'Hypnotize' quotes it, and it turns up in pop records decades later.\n\nFor something made by two people and a microphone, its footprint is disproportionate to almost anything else from the period.",["It has been sampled on hundreds of songs","Snoop Dogg rebuilt an entire track from its lyrics"],"mic","sample",{"src":"Doug E. Fresh & Slick Rick - La Di Da Di (1985)","hits":["Snoop Dogg - Lodi Dodi","Notorious B.I.G. - Hypnotize","Miley Cyrus - We Can't Stop"]}),
("05-08","Robert Moog never intended to build a music instrument","Electronic","1934","Moog was a theremin hobbyist and engineer who built modules to order. Musicians told him what they needed and the synthesizer emerged.\n\nHe started by selling theremin kits as a teenager, out of genuine hobbyist enthusiasm rather than any commercial plan. The synthesizer developed from conversations with musicians — particularly the composer Herbert Deutsch — about what they wanted a circuit to do, and Moog built modules in response to requests.\n\nThe design decisions that made it usable came from that dialogue. Voltage control, which allows one module to govern another and is the fundamental principle of the whole instrument, was an engineering solution to a musical request. The keyboard was added because players kept asking for one; there was nothing inherent in the technology that required it.\n\nHe described himself as a toolmaker rather than a musician, and consistently deflected credit toward the people who used what he built.\n\nThe commercial history was less happy. He was a poor businessman by his own admission, lost control of his own company, and for a period was legally unable to use his own name on products.\n\nWendy Carlos's Switched-On Bach in 1968 is what made the instrument credible to musicians who had dismissed it as a novelty.",["The keyboard was added because players asked for one","He said he thought of himself as a toolmaker, not a musician"],"synth","producer",{}),
("05-09","Which 'Nothing Compares 2 U' wins: Prince or Sinéad?","Pop","1985","Prince wrote it for his side project The Family. Sinéad O'Connor's 1990 version, with its single-shot video, became definitive.\n\nThe Family's original is a full band arrangement with saxophone, buried on an album by a Prince side project that lasted one record. Almost nobody heard it, and Prince rarely performed the song himself until after O'Connor's version succeeded.\n\nHer reading strips it to a sparse arrangement and a vocal that moves from restrained to broken over four minutes. Producer Nellee Hooper's arrangement leaves an enormous amount of space, which puts all the weight on the performance.\n\nThe video is close to a single continuous shot of her face, and the tears near the end were reportedly not planned — she has said she was thinking about her mother, who had died in 1985. That accident is a large part of why the record landed the way it did.\n\nO'Connor's relationship with fame afterward was adversarial and principled. Her 1992 protest against the Catholic Church on live television cost her enormously at the time and looks quite different now.\n\nShe died in 2023. Her account of Prince's own behaviour toward her was, by her telling, frightening — the two never had a comfortable relationship over a song that made them both.",["Her tears in the video were reportedly unplanned","Prince rarely performed it before her version succeeded"],"mic","versus",{"a":"The Family / Prince (1985)","b":"Sinéad O'Connor (1990)"}),
("05-10","Bo Hansson and the rise of the bedroom studio","Electronic","1970","Long before home recording was normal, musicians began assembling private studios, shifting power away from label-owned rooms.\n\nThe economics had been fixed for decades. Recording required a studio, a studio cost money by the hour, and the label paid — which meant the label controlled access, scheduling, and ultimately what got made. An artist without a deal had essentially no route to a finished record.\n\nMultitrack tape machines becoming affordable in the seventies broke that. Once a musician could own four or eight tracks, they could work at their own pace, experiment without a meter running, and arrive at a label with something finished rather than a request.\n\nThe Swedish keyboardist Bo Hansson is an early and fairly obscure example — his Lord of the Rings album was assembled largely in a private setup — and the pattern repeats through Brian Eno, through eighties four-track cassette recording, through the sampler and the sequencer, and into the laptop.\n\nWhat changed each time was not sound quality but who was permitted to make a record.\n\nThe current end point is that professional-standard production is available on hardware most people already own. The consequence is an enormous increase in supply and a corresponding collapse in the value of distribution, which is the industry's actual problem now.",["Multitrack tape became affordable in the seventies","This lineage runs directly to laptop production today"],"tape","story",{}),
("05-11","Bob Marley survived being shot and played two days later","Reggae","1976","Gunmen wounded Marley at his Kingston home before a peace concert. He performed anyway, showing the crowd his injured arm.\n\nThe context was a Jamaican election campaign of extraordinary violence, with the two major parties operating through armed gangs in Kingston neighbourhoods. Marley had agreed to play Smile Jamaica, a free concert intended to ease tensions, and the event had been widely read as aligning him with the governing party regardless of his intentions.\n\nGunmen entered his home at 56 Hope Road two days before the show. Marley was hit in the arm and chest, his wife Rita was shot in the head, and his manager Don Taylor was seriously wounded. All survived.\n\nHe played the concert. He appeared on stage with his arm visibly injured, performed for a substantial set rather than the short appearance expected, and at one point opened his shirt and raised his arms to show the crowd the wounds.\n\nHis remark afterward — that the people trying to make this world worse were not taking a day off, so he could not either — is the most-quoted thing he ever said outside a lyric.\n\nHe left Jamaica for London shortly after and stayed away for roughly eighteen months, recording Exodus in exile.",["He said 'the people who are trying to make this world worse aren't taking a day off'","He left Jamaica for London shortly after"],"mic","story",{}),
("05-12","The Winstons' break has its own documentary","Funk","1969","Decades after 'Amen, Brother', filmmakers traced how six seconds spawned entire scenes without the band's knowledge or payment.\n\nNate Harrison's 2004 video essay Can I Get an Amen? is the best-known account, and it makes an argument beyond the anecdote: that copyright as constructed does not recognise the thing that was actually valuable. Gregory Coleman's performance — the specific way he played those bars — is not itself protected in a way that generated any income for him, while the underlying composition and the recording sit with rights holders who had no part in it.\n\nThe scale of the uncompensated use is difficult to overstate. Jungle and drum and bass are built on it essentially wholesale, Aphex Twin and Squarepusher constructed careers partly on manipulating it, and it appears across rave, hip-hop, pop, and advertising.\n\nRichard Spencer, the Winstons' leader, held the copyright and never pursued enforcement, which would have been ruinously expensive and legally uncertain across decades and jurisdictions.\n\nA UK crowdfunding campaign in 2015 raised a modest amount and handed it to him — an entirely voluntary gesture from producers who felt the debt, with no legal basis whatsoever.\n\nColeman died homeless in 2006, having received nothing.",["Copyright never protected the drummer's performance itself","Public fundraising eventually delivered some belated compensation"],"drums","sample",{"src":"The Winstons - Amen, Brother (1969)","hits":["Jungle and drum & bass wholesale","Squarepusher and Aphex Twin","Countless UK rave records"]}),
("05-13","Stevie Wonder's harmonica part was a first take","Soul","1963","'Fingertips' captured a 12-year-old improvising live, including a bassist audibly asking 'What key? What key?' as the band scrambled.\n\nThe recording is from a Motortown Revue performance, and it is genuinely chaotic. Wonder, billed as Little Stevie Wonder, finished the number, left the stage, and then came back on and started playing again — which nobody had planned for. The band that had been setting up for the next act had to rejoin mid-song.\n\nThe bass player Larry Moses had already packed up, so the previous act's bassist Joe Swift stepped in, and his shouted question — what key, what key — is audible on the released record. Motown left it in.\n\nThat decision is a large part of why the record works. It is a document of something going wrong and being rescued in real time, with a crowd audibly responding to it, and the excitement is not manufactured.\n\nIt became Motown's first live recording to reach number one, and it made Wonder, at twelve, the youngest artist to top the American chart.\n\nThe label spent the following years unsure what to do with him as his voice changed. The autonomy that produced his great run was still a decade away.",["That confused shout is on the released record","It became Motown's first live number one"],"crowd","story",{}),
("05-14","Sister Nancy's 'Bam Bam' is reggae's most reused hook","Reggae","1982","Recorded quickly over an existing riddim, it became one of the most sampled Jamaican records ever, though she saw little income for decades.\n\nThe track was cut at Channel One over the Stalag riddim, a rhythm track from 1973 that has been reused hundreds of times — the Jamaican convention of multiple artists recording over a shared instrumental, which has no real equivalent in American practice.\n\nShe was among very few women working as a deejay in a scene that was almost entirely male, and the record established a template that later dancehall vocalists followed closely.\n\nWhat happened afterward is the familiar part. The song was sampled and licensed extensively for decades — Kanye West, Jay-Z, Lauryn Hill, films, advertising — and she received essentially nothing. She has described discovering her own voice in a film while sitting in a cinema, with no prior knowledge that it had been used.\n\nShe was working as an accountant in New Jersey through much of this period.\n\nShe eventually pursued the matter and regained rights to her recording, and has since been able to tour and earn from the song. That resolution took roughly thirty years and happened because she pushed for it, not because any system corrected itself.",["She discovered her song in a film only by chance","She later won back rights to her recording"],"mic","sample",{"src":"Sister Nancy - Bam Bam (1982)","hits":["Kanye West - Famous","Jay-Z - Bam","Lauryn Hill - Lost Ones"]}),
("05-15","Brooklyn's Coney Island Baby and the doo-wop corners","Brooklyn","1958","Street-corner harmony groups rehearsed in Brooklyn subway entrances and hallways for the natural reverb, an early form of free studio time.\n\nThe acoustics were the entire point. A tiled subway stairwell or a tenement hallway is a hard-surfaced reverberant box, and singing four-part harmony into one produces a natural reverb that a group could not otherwise access. Studio time cost money nobody had; a stairwell was free and open all night.\n\nThat sound is audible on the records. Doo-wop production frequently tried to reproduce the hallway rather than the concert hall, and groups auditioned by singing under overpasses and in entryways because that was where they sounded like themselves.\n\nThe form itself was a working-class Black and Italian American street tradition across Brooklyn, Harlem, and the Bronx — a cappella harmony built on a nonsense-syllable bass line, requiring no instruments and therefore no money.\n\nMost groups never recorded. Those that did were frequently signed to small labels on terms that returned almost nothing, and the pattern of teenagers signing away rights they did not understand was close to universal.\n\nThe influence carried forward directly into soul, into Motown's vocal group arrangements, and into the harmony singing underneath a great deal of what followed.",["Tiled stairwells acted as echo chambers","Groups auditioned by singing under overpasses"],"crowd","story",{}),
("05-16","Which 'Mr. Tambourine Man' wins: Dylan or The Byrds?","Folk","1965","Dylan's original is a long acoustic reverie. The Byrds cut it to two and a half minutes with a 12-string Rickenbacker and invented folk-rock.\n\nDylan's version runs over five minutes across several verses, acoustic, unhurried, and structurally closer to a poem set to a tune than to a pop single.\n\nThe Byrds kept one verse, added a chorus at each end, put it in a different key, and built it on Roger McGuinn's twelve-string Rickenbacker with close vocal harmony. Two and a half minutes, radio-shaped, and effectively the founding record of folk-rock — a genre that then absorbed a substantial part of the American charts within a year.\n\nThe recording is not really the band. Columbia's producer used session players — the Los Angeles pool later known as the Wrecking Crew — for the instrumental track, with only McGuinn among the Byrds actually playing. The vocals are the group.\n\nThat was normal practice and it does not diminish the arrangement, which is the actual innovation: the jangling twelve-string against harmony singing became one of the most copied textures in rock, running through the Beatles' mid-period work, R.E.M., and a great deal after.\n\nDylan reportedly heard it and observed that you could dance to it, which was not something anyone had said about his songs before.",["Only one Byrd actually played on the recording","Session players filled in the rest"],"guitar","versus",{"a":"Bob Dylan (1965)","b":"The Byrds (1965)"}),
("05-17","Bill Withers was making toilet seats for airplanes","Soul","1971","Withers kept his factory job while recording 'Ain't No Sunshine', assuming music would not last. The famous repeated 'I know' was a placeholder.\n\nHe was thirty-two, had spent nine years in the Navy, and was working at a factory making aircraft lavatory fittings. He recorded his first album while still employed there, and photographs from the period show him at the studio holding his lunchbox, because he was going to work afterward.\n\nThe caution was rational. He had watched the industry chew through people and had no reason to believe he would be an exception, and he kept the job until the record was demonstrably working.\n\nThe repeated 'I know' — twenty-six times in a row — was a placeholder. He intended to write a proper verse there and was told by the people in the room, including Booker T. Jones, to leave it. It is now the most memorable thing in the song, and it works because the repetition does something a lyric could not: it enacts the obsessiveness the words are describing.\n\nHe left the industry in the mid-eighties after protracted conflict with his label about creative control, and simply stopped. He gave interviews occasionally and declined, for decades, to come back.\n\nHe died in 2020.",["He meant to write real lyrics there but was told to keep it","He brought his lunchbox to the studio"],"guitar","story",{}),
("05-18","Delia Derbyshire realized the Doctor Who theme with tape","Electronic","1963","Working without synthesizers, Derbyshire built the theme by recording single tones, cutting the tape and splicing pitches by hand.\n\nThe BBC Radiophonic Workshop had no synthesizers because none existed commercially. What it had was oscillators, tape machines, filters, and an enormous amount of patience, and every sound had to be constructed physically.\n\nDerbyshire's method for the theme was close to insane by modern standards. Individual notes were generated as single tones, recorded to tape, cut to length with a razor, and spliced together in sequence — with the length of each piece of tape determining the duration of the note and the tape speed determining its pitch. Lengths were measured with a ruler.\n\nThe bassline, the swooping effects, and the melody were all assembled this way and then synchronised across multiple machines running simultaneously, because there was no multitrack.\n\nRon Grainer wrote the melody and, on hearing what she had made of it, reportedly asked whether he had written it. He wanted to give her a co-credit and BBC policy prevented it — Workshop staff were not credited as composers. She received no on-screen credit for years.\n\nIt is one of the first pieces of electronic music heard weekly by a mass audience, and it was made by cutting up tape with a blade.",["Each note was measured with a ruler","She received no on-screen composer credit for years"],"tape","producer",{}),
("05-19","'Impeach the President' gave hip-hop its snare","Funk","1973","The Honey Drippers' record, cut by a high-school band, contains a drum intro that became a foundation of nineties New York production.\n\nThe players were teenagers. Roy C. Hammond recorded the track with a group of high school students from Long Island, and the opening bars — drums alone, before anything else enters — are what producers came for two decades later.\n\nThe snare is the specific asset. It has a particular crack and decay that sits perfectly in a mid-tempo loop, and it became the default snare sound of boom-bap New York production. Once you know it, you hear it constantly across the early nineties.\n\nThe song itself is explicitly political, released during Watergate, which is a fairly unusual origin for something that became primarily valued as raw material.\n\nIt appears on records by Nas, Digable Planets, Audio Two, Janet Jackson, and a very long list beyond, and it is one of the handful of breaks — alongside the Amen, Funky Drummer, and Apache — that effectively defined a genre's rhythmic vocabulary.\n\nHammond has been more successful than most in pursuing sample income, which puts him in a small minority among the people whose recordings hip-hop was built from.",["Its snare defines much of the boom-bap era","The players were teenagers when they recorded it"],"drums","sample",{"src":"The Honey Drippers - Impeach the President (1973)","hits":["Nas - Halftime","Digable Planets - Rebirth of Slick","Janet Jackson - That's the Way Love Goes"]}),
("05-20","Bach's music was rescued by a butcher's wrapping paper","Classical","1829","Legend holds Mendelssohn found Bach manuscripts used as packing material, sparking the revival that restored Bach to the canon.\n\nThe wrapping paper story is almost certainly embellished — versions of it attach to several composers and the documentary basis is thin. What is solid is that Bach's music had substantially fallen out of performance after his death in 1750, that he was remembered primarily as an organist and a technician rather than a composer, and that a great deal of the manuscript material sat unpublished and unperformed.\n\nMendelssohn's 1829 performance of the St Matthew Passion in Berlin is the event that changed it. He was twenty, he cut and reorchestrated the work considerably to suit the forces and tastes of the time, and the performance was a public sensation. It is generally treated as the beginning of the Bach revival.\n\nThe broader point is uncomfortable. A composer now regarded as foundational to Western music spent roughly eighty years in near-obscurity, and his return depended on a wealthy, well-connected young musician deciding to take the trouble.\n\nWhat else did not get a Mendelssohn is unanswerable, which is precisely the problem with canons. They record what survived, not what was best.",["He staged the St Matthew Passion after 100 years of neglect","Bach was known mainly as an organist in his lifetime"],"strings","story",{}),
("05-21","The Bomb Squad layered dozens of samples per track","Hip-Hop","1988","Public Enemy's producers stacked sirens, horn stabs and drums into deliberately abrasive collages, a technique legally impossible today.\n\nThe approach was maximalist and deliberately unpleasant. Hank Shocklee and the Bomb Squad built tracks from twenty or more distinct sources — James Brown fragments, sirens, squealing saxophone, speech, noise — layered until the result was dense to the point of physical discomfort. The stated intention was that the music should sound like the environment it described.\n\nIt worked because nothing was cleared. In 1988 sample clearance was not systematically enforced, so a producer could use thirty sources without a lawyer being involved, and the entire aesthetic depended on that freedom.\n\nThe legal environment closed within a few years. Once clearance became mandatory and the 2005 Bridgeport ruling removed any de minimis threshold, the arithmetic became impossible: thirty sources means sixty rights holders, any of whom can refuse.\n\nChuck D and Shocklee have both said plainly that It Takes a Nation of Millions could not be made now at any budget.\n\nThat means a specific production style is not merely out of fashion but legally foreclosed — a window of about five years in which a form of music was possible and after which it was not.",["Some tracks contain over 20 distinct sources","Clearance costs would now exceed any budget"],"sampler","producer",{}),
("05-22","'Trans-Europe Express' became the spine of electro","Electronic","1977","Afrika Bambaataa built 'Planet Rock' on Kraftwerk's melody and a TR-808, connecting Düsseldorf to the Bronx.\n\nThe combination was audacious. Bambaataa took the melody from Kraftwerk's 'Trans-Europe Express', the rhythm from their 'Numbers', put both over a Roland TR-808, and produced a record that sounded like nothing before it. The connection it made — between German electronic art music and Bronx block party culture — was not obvious to anyone except the DJs who had been playing Kraftwerk records to dancers for years.\n\nThat is the part often missed. Kraftwerk were already popular in the Bronx. 'Trans-Europe Express' worked on a dancefloor, and DJs treated it as functional party music rather than as European conceptualism.\n\nThe rights position was not settled in advance, and Kraftwerk's publishers pursued the matter afterward, resulting in a settlement.\n\nWhat 'Planet Rock' created was electro, essentially whole: 808 drums, synthesizer melody, vocoder, and a rhythmic feel distinct from both funk and disco. Miami bass, Detroit techno, and a substantial portion of subsequent dance music descend from it directly.\n\nIt is also the record that put the 808 at the centre of American popular music, where it has remained for forty years.",["Kraftwerk's label pursued the matter afterward","The record effectively created electro as a genre"],"synth","sample",{"src":"Kraftwerk - Trans-Europe Express (1977)","hits":["Afrika Bambaataa - Planet Rock","Countless electro records","Detroit techno's founding template"]}),
("05-23","Rosetta Tharpe played a train station gig","Gospel","1964","Promoters staged a concert on a disused British railway platform with the audience across the tracks. Tharpe arrived by carriage and played in the rain.\n\nThe event was part of the American Folk Blues and Gospel Caravan tour, and the venue was a disused station at Wilbraham Road in Manchester. Granada Television filmed it. The staging was theatrical to the point of absurdity: the performers played on one platform while the audience sat on the opposite one, across the tracks, and Tharpe was delivered to the stage in a horse-drawn carriage.\n\nIt then rained, and she played through it without stopping, in a fur coat, on a Gibson SG, performing 'Didn't It Rain' — which is either an extraordinary coincidence or an extraordinary piece of professional presence of mind.\n\nThe footage survives and is now among the most widely seen documents of her playing. It shows the technique clearly: aggressive attack, bent notes, and a command of the audience that has nothing to do with the guitar.\n\nBritish audiences of the period were far more receptive to Black American gospel and blues artists than American ones, and a number of musicians found late-career work and genuine respect touring Britain when the domestic circuit had dried up.\n\nShe was fifty at the time and had been playing electric guitar for nearly thirty years.",["The footage became one of her best-known performances","She improvised through a downpour without stopping"],"guitar","story",{}),
("05-24","Bob Dylan went electric and got booed","Folk","1965","At Newport, Dylan plugged in and split the folk audience. The hostility followed him across a world tour.\n\nThe Newport Folk Festival set was short — three electric songs with members of the Paul Butterfield Blues Band — and the accounts of what happened are genuinely contradictory. Some present insist the booing was about the sound quality and the brevity of the set rather than the electricity; others describe real hostility. Both are probably partly true.\n\nWhat is not in doubt is the reaction over the following year. The 1966 world tour, with the Hawks behind him, met organised heckling in Britain night after night, with audiences slow-clapping and shouting through the electric half.\n\nThe most famous moment was at the Free Trade Hall in Manchester, where a voice from the audience shouted 'Judas'. Dylan responded that he did not believe him, called him a liar, and told the band to play it loud. What followed was a version of 'Like a Rolling Stone' played with genuine aggression.\n\nThe underlying argument was about authenticity and ownership — a folk audience that regarded acoustic music as morally serious and amplification as commercial betrayal, and a songwriter who had no interest in being anyone's property.",["A famous heckler shouted 'Judas' in Manchester","He responded by telling the band to play louder"],"guitar","story",{}),
("05-25","Miles Davis plugged in and lost half his audience","Jazz","1969","'Bitches Brew' used electric instruments and studio editing, alienating purists while inventing fusion.\n\nThe electric instruments were only half of it. The more radical decision was structural: producer Teo Macero recorded long, loosely directed sessions and then assembled the album afterward with tape splices, looping passages, repeating sections, and constructing pieces that were never performed continuously by anyone.\n\nThat is a rock and pop production method applied to jazz, and to a substantial part of the jazz audience it was a betrayal of the form's founding principle — that a recording documents musicians playing.\n\nDavis was explicit about his motivation. He had watched Sly Stone and Jimi Hendrix reach audiences that jazz was losing, and he had no interest in curating a museum.\n\nThe personnel is remarkable in hindsight: Wayne Shorter, Chick Corea, Herbie Hancock, John McLaughlin, Joe Zawinul, Jack DeJohnette. Nearly all of them went on to lead the fusion movement the album started.\n\nIt sold better than any jazz record he had made and cost him a substantial part of his existing audience.\n\nHe did this repeatedly across his career — bebop, cool, modal, fusion — and each time the people who had followed him to the last position objected to the next.",["Producer Teo Macero spliced the tapes like a film editor","Long passages were assembled, not performed continuously"],"horn","story",{}),
("05-26","Which 'Killing Me Softly' wins: Flack or the Fugees?","Soul","1973","Roberta Flack's version is intimate and slow. The Fugees rebuilt it in 1996 over a hip-hop break with Lauryn Hill's lead.\n\nFlack's is the definitive reading for a generation: slow, close, with an arrangement that recedes almost entirely behind the voice. She heard Lori Lieberman's original on a plane, worked on her interpretation for months, and the result is a masterclass in restraint — she never pushes, and the song is more devastating for it.\n\nThe Fugees kept the melody essentially unchanged and rebuilt everything around it, putting Lauryn Hill's lead over a hip-hop drum loop with the group's vocals responding around her. It is a cover in the most literal sense and it functions as a completely different record.\n\nWhat makes it work is that they did not try to out-sing Flack. The arrangement supplies the energy and Hill's delivery stays conversational, which leaves the song's intimacy intact inside a much bigger production.\n\nFlack praised the version publicly and without qualification, which is not the standard response of an artist watching a younger group take a signature song.\n\nIt introduced the song to an audience that had not been born when the original charted, and both versions now circulate simultaneously — which is the best outcome a cover can produce.",["Flack publicly praised the remake","The Fugees kept the melody almost unchanged"],"mic","versus",{"a":"Roberta Flack (1973)","b":"Fugees (1996)"}),
("05-27","Brooklyn's Fulton Street record shops fed the DJs","Brooklyn","1985","Independent shops along Fulton and Flatbush stocked breaks, imports and white labels that defined what Brooklyn DJs could play.\n\nThe shops were infrastructure, not retail. In a period before any digital distribution, what a DJ could play was determined entirely by what was physically available within travelling distance, and the independent shops along Fulton and Flatbush were where Brooklyn's supply came from — breaks, Jamaican imports, promotional white labels, and records the chains would never stock.\n\nShop owners functioned as informal A&R. They knew what was selling, who was buying, and which unknown record was being asked for repeatedly, and that information moved faster than any label's research. A recommendation from behind the counter could put a record into a dozen DJ crates in a week.\n\nThe shops were also social. Crews formed in the aisles, DJs met producers, and the flow of information about what was coming ran through those rooms.\n\nAlmost all of them are gone. The combination of digital distribution, rising commercial rents along both corridors, and the collapse of physical singles removed the economic basis within about a decade.\n\nWhat was lost with them is harder to replace than the records: a physical place where the people who cared about this music encountered each other by default.",["Shop owners often acted as informal A&R","Many crews formed in the aisles"],"turntable","story",{}),
("05-28","Gladys Knight had the first 'Grapevine' hit, then lost it","Soul","1967","Gladys Knight and the Pips charted with 'I Heard It Through the Grapevine' before Marvin Gaye's slower version eclipsed it entirely.\n\nGaye actually recorded it first, in early 1967. Motown's quality control committee — a real internal body that voted on which records the label released — rejected it, and the song was given to Gladys Knight and the Pips, whose faster, gospel-driven version went to number two.\n\nGaye's take sat on the shelf for over a year and was eventually released as an album track. Radio programmers found it themselves and began playing it, and the demand became large enough that Motown had no choice but to issue it as a single.\n\nIt became the label's biggest-selling record to that point.\n\nThe two versions are a good demonstration of what tempo does to meaning. Knight's is defiant and propulsive — a person confronting a betrayal with energy. Gaye's is slow, sparse, and paranoid, and the arrangement leaves so much space that the vocal sounds genuinely isolated.\n\nThe song was written by Norman Whitfield and Barrett Strong, and Whitfield had pushed hard for the Gaye version against the committee's judgement. He was right, and the committee's process — which normally worked well — produced one of the label's clearest misjudgements.",["Motown shelved Gaye's take for over a year","Gaye's version became the label's biggest-selling single to that point"],"mic","versus",{"a":"Gladys Knight & the Pips (1967)","b":"Marvin Gaye (1968)"}),
("05-29","Stravinsky's premiere caused a riot","Classical","1913","'The Rite of Spring' provoked shouting and fistfights at its Paris debut. The dancers could not hear the orchestra over the noise.\n\nThe disturbance began early and escalated. Accounts describe shouting, whistling, objects thrown, and physical fights in the aisles, with the police called at some point during the performance. The noise became loud enough that the dancers could not hear the orchestra, and Nijinsky, the choreographer, stood in the wings shouting counts at them.\n\nHow much of it was the music and how much the choreography is still argued. Stravinsky's score is genuinely violent — irregular metre, brutal dissonance, a bassoon opening in a register at the top of its range — but Nijinsky's choreography was equally confrontational: turned-in feet, stamping, and a deliberate rejection of everything classical ballet had established.\n\nThere is also a reasonable case that a portion of the audience arrived expecting a scandal and supplied one.\n\nThe work was performed again shortly afterward without incident, and it is now standard repertoire, played by student orchestras and used in a Disney film.\n\nWhat the episode preserves is a genuine cultural rupture point — a moment when an audience understood clearly that something had changed and objected in real time.",["The choreographer shouted counts from the wings","It is now standard repertoire worldwide"],"strings","story",{}),
("05-30","The SP-1200 forced producers to be brief","Hip-Hop","1987","The sampler held only about ten seconds of audio, so producers chopped tightly and sped samples up, creating a gritty sound now sought deliberately.\n\nThe memory limit was around ten seconds total, split across banks. That is not enough for a loop of any length, so producers developed a workaround: record the sample at a faster tape speed so it takes up less memory, then pitch it back down on playback. That process degrades the audio, and the degradation is the sound.\n\nThe machine's twelve-bit converters added the rest. Twelve-bit resolution is coarse by any modern standard and introduces a specific grit and compression to the low end, which producers came to want rather than tolerate.\n\nThe constraint shaped the entire aesthetic of early-nineties New York production. Short, tightly chopped samples, hard swing, and a slightly dirty texture are all consequences of a machine that could not do more.\n\nDJ Premier, Pete Rock, Large Professor, and RZA all built their signatures on it.\n\nModern plugins now emulate those limitations deliberately — twelve-bit reduction, memory-constrained workflow, the specific filter curve — which is a strange endpoint. Producers with unlimited resources pay to reintroduce restrictions that everyone in 1987 was working around.",["Its 12-bit conversion added the crunch producers loved","Modern plugins emulate its limitations on purpose"],"sampler","producer",{}),
("05-31","'Ashley's Roachclip' hid a break at the very end","Funk","1974","The Soul Searchers buried a drum break near the track's close, which producers found decades later and used everywhere.\n\nIt sits near the end of the track, after several minutes of music that gives no indication it is coming. Finding it required listening to the whole thing, which is precisely the work that crate digging consisted of — hours of unpromising records played through in full for the possibility of a few usable seconds.\n\nThe break itself has a distinctive tambourine layered over the drums, which is what makes it instantly identifiable once you know it.\n\nIt drives Eric B. & Rakim's 'Paid in Full', PM Dawn's 'Set Adrift on Memory Bliss', and Milli Vanilli's 'Girl You Know It's True' — which means one buried passage from a modestly selling 1974 record underpins both a foundational hip-hop album track and one of the biggest and most notorious pop hits of the eighties.\n\nThe Soul Searchers were led by Chuck Brown, who went on to essentially invent go-go, the Washington DC funk style built on continuous percussion and call-and-response.\n\nThe economics of all this were, as usual, one-directional. The value created by the break vastly exceeded anything that flowed back to the people who played it.",["Digging often meant listening to full albums for seconds of usable audio","The break drives one of the biggest pop hits of the eighties"],"drums","sample",{"src":"The Soul Searchers - Ashley's Roachclip (1974)","hits":["Eric B. & Rakim - Paid in Full","Milli Vanilli - Girl You Know It's True","PM Dawn - Set Adrift on Memory Bliss"]}),
]
